  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday...\n\nPatchy fog may develop early this morning amongst the river \nvalleys and lower elevations east of Billings. The latest HiRes \nmodels depict the greatest visibility reductions to far southeast \nMontana and northeastern Sheridan County. Will continue to \nmonitor this through the morning hours.\n\nA very active weekend of weather remains on track across the \nregion. Flood Watches remain in effect for all counties except for\nFallon, Custer, and Carter Counties, beginning this evening and \nlasting through the day Sunday.\n\nA deepening upper low is slowly moving into the region over the \nnext few days. This will bring very high precipitable water values\n(200 to 250% of normal) through the weekend. At the surface, \nsoutheasterly flow will contribute to the high PWAT values, as \nGulf and Plains moisture is advected into the region. Showers and \nthunderstorms will begin to develop this afternoon, becoming \nwidespread overnight and lasting into Sunday.\n\nThe risk for severe thunderstorms today is currently low, with \nthe latest SPC outlook depicting a general risk of thunderstorms \nacross much of Montana and Wyoming. There are two areas of \ninterest for a few stronger storms this afternoon. The first area \nis southwestern MT where the greatest upper diffluence is shown by\nmodels. Models do show MUCAPE values climbing towards 1000 J/kg \nas well. Something to note however, is that the latest HREF \ndepicts stronger storms further west than into Park County. The \nsecond area of stronger storm potential exists mainly east of \nBillings where MUCAPE values are shown approaching 2000 J/kg. Low-\nlevel shear values are going to be fairly weak (generally less \nthan 15 knots) across much of the area while mid- layer shear will\nbe modest (20 to 40 knots). For both areas, the main threats are \ngusty winds, heavy to very heavy rainfall, and hail. Those with \noutdoor and sports viewing plans should pay attention to the \nforecast and be prepared in case a strong storm develops in your \nvicinity tomorrow. \n\nThe probability for at least an inch of rainfall tonight through \nSunday is 75 to 100% for most of south-central Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming. Meanwhile, while the chance over far eastern \nMontana is currently 30 to 60%. The probability for at least 2 \ninches of rainfall is 40 to 80% from Rosebud County and west, \ngreatest around and west of Billings. The chance for stronger \nthunderstorms exists south and east of Billings on Saturday. For \nthe rest of the area, the risk of stronger storms will be low with\nwidespread showers and embedded thunderstorms expected. The risk \nfor locally very heavy rainfall exists as well, with the latest \nrun of the REFS 3-hour QPF Max showing pockets of 2-4+ inches of \nrain in 3 hour periods on Saturday.\n\nWhile much of the rain will fall over the course of the weekend, \nthe risk for locally higher amounts warranted the aforementioned \nflood watches. Recent burn scars will be highly susceptible for \nflash flooding and debris flows in the current long duration \nprecipitation forecast, especially if thunderstorm precipitation \nimpacts them. Those living near recent burn scars should have a \nplan of action to seek safety should the heavy rainfall develop in\nthose locations. Pay attention to local officials directions and \nfor any Flash Flood Warnings that may be issued. \n\nPrecipitation should taper off Sunday night into Monday from west\nto east as the upper low moves into the Northern Plains states. \nWhile much drier weather is forecast for next week, depending on \nhow much rain falls this weekend, there will probably be a good \nchance for at least afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms each day into mid-week just from the residual \nsurface moisture across the region. \n\nSnowfall...With the increased precipitation forecast, snow \namounts in the highest peaks of area mountains have climbed into \nthe 3 to 6 inch range. Snow levels start out around 12kft tonight,\ndropping to 11kft Saturday afternoon and possibly falling to \naround 10kft Saturday night. Currently, it appears unlikely that a\nWinter Weather Advisory will be needed for the Beartooth Pass. \nThe most likely timeframe where one could be needed though would \nbe Saturday night into Sunday morning. That said, anyone planning \ntravel over the pass this weekend should be prepared for winter \nconditions. \n\nHigh temperatures are forecast in the 70s to mid 80s today, \nwarmest in southeast Montana. Over the weekend, temperatures look \nto be in the 60s before climbing back into the 70s by Monday.\n\nMatos/Chambers",
